extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Features | Tooltips"
	- pagedescription = "Tooltips in Tablechart: the values of a row, when you point at it or tap it."

block content
	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Tooltips
				p.lead The values of a row, when you point at it or tap it.

	+section("When a chart has a tooltip")
		p If a chart does not show all its numbers (when it gets too crowded), you need a tooltip.  Hovering a row, or tapping it, then shows a tooltip with the name of the row and its values. With several value columns, each value has the name of its column and a key in its colour. A stack also shows its total.
		p A chart that shows all its numbers has no tooltip: it would only repeat the number you point at, and cover it. If the chart gets narrower and leaves numbers out, its tooltip comes on.
		p This chart has three lines close together, so it leaves numbers out. If you point at it, the tooltip shows.

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="line" data-chart-legend)
				figcaption: b Apples per tree
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Year
							th North
							th South
							th East
					tbody
						each row in [['2018', '41', '38', '35'], ['2019', '45', '44', '42'], ['2020', '48', '47', '49'], ['2021', '52', '50', '51'], ['2022', '55', '56', '53'], ['2023', '58', '57', '59'], ['2024', '61', '60', '62'], ['2025', '64', '66', '63']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]
								td= row[3]

	+section("How it works")
		ul
			li The tooltip uses the names and numbers of the chart, so it has the same number format and the same translations.
			li It goes above the row, or below it if there is no room above. It stays inside the window.
			li Pressing any key, tapping somewhere else, or moving the pointer away closes the tooltip. 
			li Only a mouse that moves opens a tooltip. If the page changes under a mouse that rests, for example when a key shows the next slide of a presentation or the page scrolls, no tooltip opens.
			li The text has the size of the chart's text on the screen, also when the chart is scaled.
			li On a chart with steps, a row that has not arrived yet has no tooltip.
			li Screen readers do not read the tooltip. They read the table, which has the same values.

	+section("Always or never")
		p If you want a tooltip all the time, also when a chart shows all its numbers, add #[code data-chart-tooltip="true"] to the figure. This chart shows all its numbers, and has a tooltip anyway.

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="column" data-chart-tooltip="true")
				figcaption: b Apples picked
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Spring', '58'], ['Summer', '72'], ['Autumn', '70'], ['Winter', '41']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

		p #[code data-chart-tooltip="false"] turns it off. If you want to set it for every chart, use the #[code tooltip] option: #[code "auto"] by default, #[code true], #[code false] or #[code "crosshair"].

		+codeblock('JS').
			init({ tooltip: true });

	+section("Crosshair")
		-
			const monthNames = ['Jan', 'Feb', 'Mar', 'Apr', 'May', 'Jun', 'Jul', 'Aug', 'Sep', 'Oct', 'Nov', 'Dec'];
			// Three years of the price of a kilo of apples at two markets: a slow rise, with the seasons in it.
			const markets = Array.from({ length: 36 }, (_, i) => {
				const season = Math.cos(((i % 12) - 1) / 6 * Math.PI);
				const north = 1.6 + i * 0.012 + season * 0.18 + (((i * 7) % 5) - 2) * 0.02;
				const south = 1.45 + i * 0.015 + season * 0.12 + (((i * 3) % 4) - 1.5) * 0.025;
				return [`${monthNames[i % 12]} ${2023 + Math.floor(i / 12)}`, String(2023 + Math.floor(i / 12)), north.toFixed(2), south.toFixed(2)];
			});
		p With #[code data-chart-tooltip="crosshair"], a line or area chart shows a crosshair instead of a tooltip: a thin line down through the row you point at, and one across through the point nearest to the pointer. Each line gets a dot, and its value at the side of the value axis, in the colour of its line. The name of the row is under the line down. Values that would touch are pushed apart.
		p Together with #[code data-chart-values="none"] and #[code data-chart-axis], the chart shows clean lines, and exact values only where you point. This chart has #[code data-chart-axis="right"], so the values go over the numbers on the right. See #[a(href=url('/styling/axes.html')) Axes and gridlines].

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="line" data-chart-tooltip="crosshair" data-chart-values="none" data-chart-axis="right" data-chart-grid data-chart-legend data-chart-scale="1.2 2.2" data-chart-shape="smooth")
				figcaption
					b The price of apples
					br
					span euro per kilo
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Month
							th North
							th(data-line="dashed") South
					tbody
						each row in markets
							tr
								th(data-short=row[1])= row[0]
								td= row[2]
								td= row[3]

		+codeblock('HTML').
			<figure data-chart="line" data-chart-tooltip="crosshair" data-chart-values="none" data-chart-axis data-chart-grid data-chart-scale="1.2 2.2">

		p On other chart types, #[code crosshair] gives the normal tooltip. If you want a crosshair on every line and area chart, set the #[code tooltip] option to #[code "crosshair"].
		p As the tooltip, the crosshair works with a pointer or a tap. It is not printed, and screen readers read the table instead.
		p The line of the crosshair is #[code --tablechart-crosshair-color] and #[code --tablechart-crosshair-width], and the size of its text is #[code --tablechart-crosshair-size].

	+section("Style it")
		p The background, text, border, corners and shadow of the tooltip are CSS variables. See #[a(href=url('/styling/tooltips.html')) Styling tooltips], with examples.

	+section("Next")
		p See #[a(href=url('/features/numbers.html')) Numbers] for how numbers are shown, and #[a(href=url('/reference/variables.html')) CSS variables] for all variables.
